WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.620
Book to Movie adaptations are tricky to the
point where the phrase “the book was better”

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:05.839
has become a cliche.

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:09.250
The book, The Disaster Artist, is one of my
favorite books about filmmaking.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:13.799
It’s about Tommy Wiseau, the infamous creator
of The Room and is written by Tommy’s best

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:15.990
friend and co-star Greg Sestero.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.740
The book is not only the behind the scenes
tale of the making of what has been dubbed

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.539
“the greatest bad movie of all time”,
it’s the story of the strange, eccentric,

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:26.260
mysterious man behind the film.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:31.259
So naturally I was interested when I found
out that James Franco was directing an adaptation

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:32.450
of Greg’s Book.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:36.340
And the first question I had about the film,
was what will Tommy think of the movie?

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:48.780
We eventually got the answer, which surprised
me, since famous Tommy only approved of Greg’s

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:56.900
book 40%.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:59.610
So why does Tommy love the movie and dislike
the book?

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.870
Is it just another strange Tommy Wiseau quirk?

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:06.480
I don’t think so, and in fact I think the
disparity lies at the heart of why Franco’s

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.510
movie is not a good adaptation.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:16.720
When it comes to adaptations fans often get
upset about small changes or “inaccuracies”.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:23.000
In a strange way, The Disaster Artist is obsessed
with accuracy on a detail level.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:32.390
Many scenes are fairly close to what’s in
the book.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:37.960
And as the movie proudly shows us at the end,
much care was taken to accurately re-create

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.570
actual scenes from The Room.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:44.830
Franco’s performance as Tommy is also surprisingly
good.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:51.810
It’s about as accurate an embodiment of
Tommy as we could ask for.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:59.330
And yet to me something is still felt… off.

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:02.630
The Disaster Artist is accurate on a micro
scale.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:07.770
But I think the changes is does make shift
the heart of the story in a way that is interesting,

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.060
but ultimately detrimental to the story.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:15.430
The Tommy Greg illustrates for us in the book
is difficult to understand and deal with.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:17.209
He’s complicated and fascinating.

00:02:17.209 --> 00:02:20.980
He’s a stubborn hopeful who is mysteriously
wealthy.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:26.330
Greg’s depiction of Tommy is certainly caring
and sympathetic, but it’s also laced with

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:36.700
honesty about the darker side of the story,
it’s not afraid to criticize it’s subject.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:41.920
Franco’s Tommy retains some of these qualities,
but ultimately distills Tommy down into a

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.390
sort of underdog hero.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:49.180
Franco’s Tommy is a quirky guy who sticks
to his dream and wins against all odds.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:03:08.670
And Franco’s movie celebrates that.

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:19.030
Sure it highlights some of the frustrating
Tommy moments and 

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:23.410
the crew’s exasperation, but it does so
mostly for comedic effect.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:34.330
While in the film tension between Greg and
Tommy is a plot point late in the second act.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:39.700
In the book, this tension is at the core of
what makes the story so interesting.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.459
The movie displays Greg’s participation
and cheerleader attitude without revealing

00:03:44.459 --> 00:03:49.099
the internal struggle and self-awareness that
accompanied that.

00:03:49.099 --> 00:04:01.830
In the movie Greg is simplified to someone
who worships Tommy’s self-confidence, 

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:06.450
and only really second-guesses things when
Tommy gets in the way of his career.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:21.579
In the book Greg refuses to be in The Room
at first, only eventually giving in.

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:24.720
Missing from the film is a close examination
of Greg.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.809
Why does Greg, knowing things are bad, continue
to enable and support Tommy?

00:04:29.809 --> 00:04:31.749
Is he just being a good friend?

00:04:31.749 --> 00:04:36.699
Is blindly encouraging someone when you know
what they’re making isn’t good being a

00:04:36.699 --> 00:04:43.379
good friend?

00:04:43.379 --> 00:05:00.400
Greg even questions himself throughout 

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.009
the book.

00:05:03.009 --> 00:05:07.340
The movie overlooks some of the darker themes
and trauma that may have led to Tommy’s

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:08.909
creation of the room.

00:05:08.909 --> 00:05:14.009
In the book Tommy disappears for a while,
Greg, unable to get in contact with him, is

00:05:14.009 --> 00:05:16.979
quite worried something bad may have happened.

00:05:16.979 --> 00:05:20.289
Tommy eventually re-appears and decides to
film The Room.

00:05:20.289 --> 00:05:25.719
Gone from this movie is Tommy’s dark night
of the soul, it’s reduced to a sort of feel-good

00:05:25.719 --> 00:05:39.199
pep-talk.

00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:45.249
And in one of the most tonally dissonant moments
I’ve seen a film in a while Franco’s movie

00:05:45.249 --> 00:05:50.270
presents a theater full of people chanting
for a character to kill themselves, and expects

00:05:50.270 --> 00:06:04.030
us to accept it as a positive thing.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:17.919
Look at how Greg describes the story he tells
in the book, compared with how Franco describes

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:21.690
the story he’s telling in the movie.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:38.669
Greg’s book is a tragedy.

00:06:38.669 --> 00:06:41.879
It doesn’t give us a happy ending.

00:06:41.879 --> 00:06:46.340
We the audience, with hindsight, know Tommy
will eventually become accepted and loved,

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:49.249
though not in the way Tommy wanted.

00:06:49.249 --> 00:06:58.520
The movie is a comedy, it treats it’s story
as the classic “if you dream big and work

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:03.599
hard you’ll succeed” story but with a
twist.

00:07:03.599 --> 00:07:13.610
It’s celebrates Tommy’s success, and shows
Tommy accepting the backwards nature of his

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:29.569
success without much struggle.

00:07:29.569 --> 00:07:34.349
While the Movie might be accurate about a
lot of things, the heart of the story is completely

00:07:34.349 --> 00:07:35.349
different.

00:07:35.349 --> 00:07:38.139
It’s a poor adaptation in the macro sense.

00:07:38.139 --> 00:07:43.279
This oversimplification of Tommy into a hero
character I think is obviously at the heart

00:07:43.279 --> 00:07:45.699
of Tommy’s love of the film.

00:07:45.699 --> 00:07:49.309
Tommy loves the movie because it presents
him in the best possible light.

00:07:49.309 --> 00:07:50.309
But by doing so the story is stripped of it’s
humanity.

00:07:50.309 --> 00:07:51.642
And I get it, it’s nice to imagine Tommy
this way.

00:07:51.642 --> 00:07:55.080
It’s nice to imagine that we live in a world
where if you’re bad at what you do you’ll

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:57.349
still end up successful, famous, and happy.

00:07:57.349 --> 00:07:59.599
That’s the fun story to tell.

00:07:59.599 --> 00:08:02.409
And maybe in this case it’s the true one.

00:08:02.409 --> 00:08:08.360
There’s another piece to this puzzle, The
Disaster Artist, book and movie, are both

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:10.379
“Adaptations” of real life.

00:08:10.379 --> 00:08:13.960
So while the movie is partly an adaptation
of the book, it’s also and adaptation of

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:17.990
Franco and the writer’s perspective on the
real life story.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:22.649
Greg’s book isn’t the definitive truth
and it’s possible that it is colored unnecessarily

00:08:22.649 --> 00:08:26.979
dark by his personal involvement and subjective
experience.

00:08:26.979 --> 00:08:32.110
This is a revealing example of how difficult
good adaptation really is.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:35.770
I don’t think Franco’s movie is a dishonest
adaptation.

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:37.390
It doesn’t lie about Tommy and the story.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:38.390
But by choosing what to include and leave
out, and changing the tone of the presentation

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:39.390
you can create a very different story.

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:43.370
But personally I find the book to be much
more interesting and thought provoking.

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:48.010
The movie wants us to believe there’s something
about Tommy that makes The Room, not just

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:50.740
a terrible movie, but a special movie.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:51.910
And I agree.

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:55.720
But it only explores Tommy on a surface level.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:01.910
And in a strange way I feel like the movie
is the sort of plastic, fake, story that Tommy

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:04.940
is so quick to criticize.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:10.720
I originally listened to Greg’s book as
an audiobook on a road trip, and the trip

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:11.820
